// Executable is an authority, not a default (spec §2.5, §3).
//
// X is a property of the mapping; which access bits a handle may request is a
// right. A region carries no R/W/X triple, which lets one region be read-only
// here and read-write there, and `MemoryRight.MapExecute` says whether this
// handle may ask for `MapAccess.Execute` at all.
//
// The transcript reads as an order:
//
//   SOS execgate: mapped executable                       <- root's full handle
//   SOS execgate: minted a sibling without MapExecute
//   SOS execgate: the sibling maps read-write             <- it is a live handle
//   SOS execgate: asking for execute through it
//   SOS: process fault: access denied
//
// The third line is what makes the fourth mean anything. A sibling that could
// not map at all would produce the same refusal for the wrong reason, so this
// program maps read-write through the narrowed handle first and only then asks
// for execute. One handle, two asks, one refusal: the difference is the bit.
//
// This is capability flow, not identity. The kernel never asks who is calling.
// Root maps executable because root's boot pool carries the bit and hands it
// down (`pool_rights()` mints it, and has to: attenuation is monotonic, so a
// bit not minted at boot could never appear later); the sibling cannot
// because the mask that made it left the bit out. "Only root maps executable"
// is something a launcher arranges once, at the mint where it hands memory
// over, not a rule enforced per call.
//
// The mask below is what a launcher would write for a driver child's RAM,
// applied to a sibling root keeps. That is the only way to observe the refusal
// in one transcript: a rights refusal is a fault, so it ends whichever process
// meets it, and a launcher meeting its own child's refusal would read it out of
// a §8 status word instead of off the console.
//
// W|X is a different claim, covered by `map-wx-refused`: this case asks "may
// you produce an executable row at all", that one asks "may one row be both
// writable and executable". They are two cases because each is a fault and a
// fault ends the process.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, Pool, Mapping, MapAccess, MemoryRight,
            SosStatus}

/// One page of the pool, which is the smallest thing either profile grants.
static CHUNK: UInt = 0x1000

/// Read and execute: a text mapping, the one access this case gates.
static RX: UInt32 = MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Execute

/// Read and write, which is what any scratch region wants and what the narrowed
/// handle is still perfectly able to install.
static RW: UInt32 = MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Write

/// The policy a launcher writes for a child's RAM. Keep the ticket that lets
/// the region travel (`Transfer`, the bit `give` checks) and the authority to
/// install it (`Map`). `MapExecute` is absent by silence, the keep mask's
/// polarity: what is not named is not kept, so a bit added to `MemoryRight`
/// later is denied to every child configured with this mask. `Split` is absent
/// too, so the child may not subdivide its RAM for anyone.
static CHILD_RAM: UInt32 = (MemoryRight.Transfer | MemoryRight.Map)

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // One boot region: the RAM pool. Root's config is what says so.
    var pool_record = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS execgate: no pool region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let pool = pool_record.take_pool() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: pool record is not a pool\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let page = try pool.allocate(len: CHUNK) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: allocation failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // Positive arm: root maps executable, and the row installing is the proof.
    // Nothing is executed out of this page (a page of uninitialised pool is not
    // a program), and asserting a jump would assert the loader rather than the
    // gate. What is claimed is that a handle carrying `MemoryRight.MapExecute`
    // may ask for `MapAccess.Execute`, which is a row the kernel installs or a
    // fault it raises, and one of those two things happens on the next line.
    let _text = try proc.map(memory: &page, access: RX) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mapping executable failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: mapped executable\n")

    // The narrowing is the ordinary universal mint: a sibling onto the same
    // region, in root's own table, carrying root's rights intersected with the
    // mask. Root's original handle is untouched and still maps executable. That
    // is what "per handle, not per region" means (see `MemoryRight`): a second
    // handle that still carries the bit can map the same bytes executable,
    // because there is no aliasing bookkeeping.
    let narrow = try page.mint(rights: CHILD_RAM) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mint failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: minted a sibling without MapExecute\n")

    // The control. The narrowed handle is a working Memory handle: it maps
    // read-write, over the same page, into the same domain. Without this line
    // the refusal below would be consistent with the sibling being broken in
    // any of a dozen ways.
    let _data = try proc.map(memory: &narrow, access: RW) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mapping read-write failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: the sibling maps read-write\n")

    // The refusal is a fault and therefore the last thing this program does.
    // An unspent right ends the caller, the same answer every other one gives,
    // and it is caller-checkable: rights are a property of the handle it chose
    // to call through, and this program minted it.
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: asking for execute through it\n")
    match proc.map(memory: &narrow, access: RX) {
        case Ok(_) -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: UNREACHABLE\n")
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
        case Err(e) -> {
            print("SOS execgate: UNREACHABLE status {}\n", e)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
    }
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
}
